From a7d1bfa521b350388818267c5955a4f92e1a8476 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Swiftly <accounts@paulherter.de>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 02:17:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vout, clock: make pause take effect immediately
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two halves of the same problem, measured on macOS.

1. clock: vlc_clock_main_ChangePause() set main_clock->paused and returned
   without waking anyone. A thread in vlc_clock_Wait() therefore slept until
   its original deadline — for the video output the presentation date of the
   next picture, up to 41 ms at 24 fps. The resume path already ends in a
   broadcast; only the pause path returned early without one.

2. vout: once awake, the display loop set its deadline to
   now + VOUT_REDISPLAY_DELAY (80 ms), waited it out and displayed the
   pending picture on the timeout.

Together the image kept advancing for up to ~120 ms after the user pressed
pause, varying with frame rate and with where the keypress landed between two
frames. The picture is still displayed in both cases, so the periodic
redisplay VOUT_REDISPLAY_DELAY exists for is unaffected — only the waiting is
gone.
---
 src/clock/clock.c               | 13 +++++++++++++
 src/video_output/video_output.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 38 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/clock/clock.c b/src/clock/clock.c
index bb137d67f9..006c87a789 100644
--- a/src/clock/clock.c
+++ b/src/clock/clock.c
@@ -1058,6 +1058,19 @@ void vlc_clock_main_ChangePause(vlc_clock_main_t *main_clock, vlc_tick_t now,
     if (paused)
     {
         main_clock->pause_date = now;
+        /* Swiftly: wake everyone waiting on this clock.
+         *
+         * Without this, a thread sitting in vlc_clock_Wait() keeps sleeping
+         * until its original deadline — for the video output that is the
+         * presentation date of the next picture, up to 41 ms for 24 fps
+         * content. It only notices the pause once it wakes up on its own, so
+         * the image kept moving after the user asked for a pause, by an
+         * amount that varied with the frame rate and with where the keypress
+         * landed between two frames.
+         *
+         * The resume path below already ends in a broadcast; only the pause
+         * path returned early without one. */
+        vlc_cond_broadcast(&main_clock->cond);
         return;
     }
 
diff --git a/src/video_output/video_output.c b/src/video_output/video_output.c
index 90a0bcd617..1e2a303cec 100644
--- a/src/video_output/video_output.c
+++ b/src/video_output/video_output.c
@@ -1493,7 +1493,31 @@ static int RenderPicture(vout_thread_sys_t *sys,
             {
                 vlc_tick_t deadline;
                 if (vlc_clock_IsPaused(sys->clock))
-                    deadline = max_deadline;
+                {
+                    /* Swiftly: paused — show it now, do not wait.
+                     *
+                     * The original code set the deadline to now +
+                     * VOUT_REDISPLAY_DELAY (80 ms) and waited it out, then
+                     * displayed the picture on the timeout. Pressing pause
+                     * therefore let the image advance one more frame up to
+                     * 80 ms later — at 120 Hz that is up to ten frames, and
+                     * where the keypress lands inside that window decides how
+                     * bad it looks. That is the "it keeps playing after I hit
+                     * pause" our users report.
+                     *
+                     * Measured on macOS, from the click: VLCKit's dispatch
+                     * queue 17-25 ms (patched separately), libvlc's own pause
+                     * path 157-216 us, and then this: up to 80 ms.
+                     *
+                     * Displaying right away still advances by the single
+                     * picture that was already rendered — about 8 ms of
+                     * over-run instead of up to 80. The periodic redisplay
+                     * that VOUT_REDISPLAY_DELAY exists for (window resize and
+                     * friends) is unaffected: the picture is still displayed,
+                     * just without the wait. */
+                    system_pts = vlc_tick_now();
+                    break;
+                }
                 else
                 {
                     assert(!sys->displayed.current->b_force);
-- 
